## Why this exists

`AuthGuard` uses `useAuthConfig()` to know whether Azure auth is actually configured. When it isn't (no client/tenant ID from `/api/config`), it falls into a **dev bypass** instead of forcing a login. The value is provided by `AuthProvider`, which wraps the app in an `AuthConfigContext.Provider value={{ authConfigured: ... }}`.

So two things to verify in your work copy:

1. **`lib/authContext.ts`** exports `useAuthConfig` + `AuthConfigContext` (the block above). ← this is what fixes your red underline.
2. **`AuthProvider.tsx`** actually wraps its children with `<AuthConfigContext.Provider value={{ authConfigured }}>` and sets `authConfigured` based on whether `clientId`/`tenantId` came back from `/api/config`. If your `AuthProvider` doesn't do this, `authConfigured` will always be the default `false` and you'll be stuck in dev bypass.

