I've traced the full token path in this copy and it's wired correctly:

- `apiFetch` (lib/api.ts) calls `getAccessToken()`, and **only** attaches `Authorization: Bearer <token>` *if a token comes back* (line 65: `if (token)`).
- It fetches `/api/proxy/...` (API_BASE_URL is `/api/proxy`).
- The proxy (app/api/proxy/[...path]/route.ts, lines 15–18) forwards the `authorization` header to FastAPI.

So a 401 with "no token reaching the backend" means the chain is breaking in one of two halves. You can tell which in 10 seconds, and the fix is different for each.

## Do this first — bisect it in the browser DevTools

Open the live app, Network tab, trigger a data call, and click the request to **`/api/proxy/v1/tables/list`**. Look at its **Request Headers** for `Authorization: Bearer …`:

**Case A — there is NO `Authorization` header on that request.**
Then `getAccessToken()` returned `null`. This is the most likely cause, and it's silent because the function swallows the error:
```ts
const result = await _instance.acquireTokenSilent({ scopes: _apiScope ? [_apiScope] : OIDC_SCOPES, account: accounts[0] });
...
} catch { return null; }   // <-- you never see why
```
`acquireTokenSilent` throws (→ null → no header → 401) when any of these is true:
- The **API scope is wrong**. The default is `api://<clientId>/access_as_user`. If your App Registration's exposed scope / App ID URI is different, set **`AZURE_API_SCOPE`** to the exact value (e.g. `api://<your-app-id-uri>/access_as_user` or `<clientId>/.default`). This is the most common culprit right after going live.
- **Consent was never granted** for that scope (admin consent), or the user's cached session predates the scope — so there's no token to return silently.

**Case B — the `Authorization` header IS present on the `/api/proxy` request, but the backend still sees none.**
Then the token dies at the proxy hop. Two sub-causes:
- Your real repo's proxy route doesn't forward `authorization` (compare it to lines 15–18 above).
- **You're deploying on Databricks Apps**, whose auth layer can consume/replace the `Authorization` header and instead injects `x-forwarded-access-token`. If your backend's `AuthMiddleware` reads `Authorization: Bearer`, the platform may be stripping it before FastAPI sees it. This is a real gotcha specific to Databricks Apps.

## Two code fixes (for Case A)

These make the failure visible and recover from it instead of silently sending no token. In `lib/auth.ts`, change `getAccessToken` to fall back to an interactive redirect when silent acquisition needs interaction:

```ts
import {
  PublicClientApplication,
  InteractionRequiredAuthError,
  type Configuration,
} from '@azure/msal-browser';

export async function getAccessToken(): Promise<string | null> {
  if (typeof window === 'undefined' || !_instance) return null;
  const accounts = _instance.getAllAccounts();
  if (!accounts.length) return null;
  const scopes = _apiScope ? [_apiScope] : OIDC_SCOPES;
  try {
    const result = await _instance.acquireTokenSilent({ scopes, account: accounts[0] });
    return result.accessToken;
  } catch (err) {
    // Surface the real reason instead of silently returning null -> 401.
    console.error('acquireTokenSilent failed', err);
    if (err instanceof InteractionRequiredAuthError) {
      await _instance.acquireTokenRedirect({ scopes });
    }
    return null;
  }
}
```

The `console.error` alone will immediately tell you whether the scope is wrong, consent is missing, or interaction is required — which removes all the guessing.

